  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Background
    
    Qeihong began in the crystal-forges of Aurora Stack, the vertical colony carved into the sunlit face of Aurora Berg. Her flippers still carry the white scarring characteristic of forge-work. The administrative record of her transition into governance is incomplete — a gap the ice has not clarified — but by the time of the Eastern Shaft collapse, she held sufficient rank to negotiate on the Stack's behalf.
    
    ## The Eastern Shaft and the Midnight Claws
    
    The collapse of the Eastern Shaft brought Qeihong into contact with the Vum∴tenebra, a deep-ice phenomenon that left her tide-touched. She emerged alone, carrying a sealed contract and intelligence that reshaped Aurora Stack's approach to the orca threat. The raiders known as the Midnight Claws, she reported, worship a dead god in the deep and raid not for plunder but to wake it. More critically: they can be bargained with.
    
    This intelligence led directly to the Foedus∴vincu, a formal compact granting the Midnight Claws diplomatic access and protected passage through the Berg's tunnels. Qeihong witnessed and co-signed the drafting. Light-Regent Xouhoulong, then leader of both Aurora Stack and the Icebound Exchange — the merchant guild controlling the Stack's trade routes — sealed it with the Directrix Mirror. Whether Qeihong understood at that stage the full scope of Xouhoulong's hidden arrangements is unclear from the record. She may have. The ice does not preserve intent.
    
    ## The Calving and Displacement of Xouhoulong
    
    Three weeks after the compact's sealing, the Calving tore open the eastern ice, killing many — including High-Beak Auditor Selka, Xouhoulong's companion and predecessor at the Exchange. Qeihong reportedly sensed the catastrophe before word reached her, a detail consistent with tide-touched sensitivity but difficult to verify.
    
    Her response was systematic. An eight-day investigation catalogued sealed galleries violating charter terms, unauthorized research into Wake-Singer pressure-magic, and accommodations with Nightshelf doctrines — specifically Mandatum∴absconditu, the creed that survival demands secrecy — that the Flipper Accord did not recognize. She confronted Xouhoulong over his invocation of a Nightshelf doctrine as justification for actions taken under Aurora authority, and displaced him through sealed transfer of Light-Regency.
    
    The investigation was thorough. Whether it was also convenient — providing legal architecture for a succession that the Calving had already made inevitable — is a question the chronicle sources do not ask.
    
    ## Governance
    
    Qeihong now chairs the Icebound Exchange and leads Aurora Stack as Grand Auditor, enforcing the Accord with full awareness of its arithmetic: how many penguins starve if the fire-cores stop flowing, how many more if the orcas breach the upper shelves. She governs through The Way∴al, the doctrine that the aurora judges fairly — a framework she promotes with conviction, or at least consistency.
    
    Her aide, Beacon-Keeper Lianhinglou, serves with a loyalty the chronicles describe as careful. The Stack's governance continues to fragment around her; the Exchange's institutional weight has diminished since the authentication scandals. Qeihong manages competing power centers with fewer resources than her predecessors commanded.
    
    She wears Xouhoulong's aurora-circlet. It sits too loose. She has not had it adjusted.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771576132957_0", anchor: "The raiders known as the Midnight Claws, she reported, worship a dead god in the deep", text: "The Midnight Claws are Nyla's Nightshelf organization — smugglers, tunnel enforcers, shadow-economy operators. They are not orca raiders. What I compressed here is a chain: Qeihong's intelligence about Wake-Singer theology came through the Claws, who had been conducting unsanctioned trade with orca intermediaries in the deep tunnels for longer than any surface authority suspected. The Foedus∴vincu named the Claws as go-betweens. The orcas were never directly at the table. They have never needed to be at any table to get what they want.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576132957_1", anchor: "Whether it was also convenient", text: "It was. The sealed galleries were real — I have been in them — but she chose the eight days after a catastrophe that killed the only person whose authority superseded hers to open them. Timing is not fabrication. Both can be true.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576132957_2", anchor: "a loyalty the chronicles describe as careful", text: "'Careful' is performing heroically in this sentence. The related materials describe a functionary who learned forbidden deep-techniques and discovered things in the ice that terrify his colleagues. His loyalty is not careful; it is *conditional* on something neither party discusses publicly. The Whisper Galleries carry an acoustic impression of two voices negotiating terms in a dialect mixing Aurora formal register with deep-pressure terminology. One has the tonal signature of tide-touch. I did not identify the other. I chose not to try very hard.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576132957_3", anchor: "She has not had it adjusted", text: "No.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
